3 Method
3.3 Fusion Architecture
To accommodate varying modality combinations during fusion, we design a two-stage fusion strategy within each universal sequence Transformer in Fig. 1 (a): (1) modalityshared fusion; (2) modality-specific decoupling. As shown in Fig. 2, we propose modality-specific mixture of experts (MoE) to decode structural characteristics of each modality from the fused latent space. This modality MoE architecture is particularly well-suited for CPath, as it enables the model to capture high-dimensional spatial features in WSIs, sparse tabular patterns in genomics, and semantic cues in clinical text.
Specifically, we employ modality-shared self-attention layers Φ( · ) to model interactions across modalities for the first stage. The shared attention matrix maps combinations of modalities into a unified latent space, Z ′ = Φ(Z) + Z , where Z = { H (3) , G (3) , T (1) } denotes the concatenation feature input and Z ′ = { H ′ , G ′ , T ′ } is the resulting output. In the next stage, we introduce modality-specific experts
