6. Ablation Study
6.3. Impact of Token Replication Factor
Figure 8 illustrates the effect of the token replication factor α on the promoter TATA classification task for Omni-DNA sized 116M and 1B. Without token replication ( α = 1 ), classification performance is poor. We find that setting α within the range [8, 11] is effective across various task types.
Figure 8. Impact of the token replication factor α on the promoter TATA classification task.

SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE

Line plot showing F1-score versus repeating factor. The x-axis is labeled “Repeating Factor” with values around 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, and 12; the y-axis is labeled “F1-Score” ranging roughly from 0.4 to 0.9+. Two curves are shown: orange “Omni-DNA 116M” and green “Omni-DNA 1B.” Both improve as repeating factor increases. Omni-DNA 116M rises quickly by factor 4 and then stays near 0.9, while Omni-DNA 1B stays low until factor 4, jumps sharply by factor 6, and remains slightly higher near 0.95 afterward.

